<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chronicle of Fredegar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 660</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Fredegar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chronicle of Fredegar is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> chronicle compiled in Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, probably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, around 660, and attributed since the 16th century to an otherwise unknown 'Fredegar'. Drawing on earlier works such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>, it continues the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> to about 642 and is the main source for the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagobert-i">Dagobert I</a> and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samo</a>'s realm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>. Later continuations carried it into the Carolingian period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar.jpg" alt="Chronicle of Fredegar" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paris,_BnF_MS_Latin_10910_fol._23v_Chronicle_of_Fredegar_-_Miniature_Eusebius,_Jerome_and_a_mallard_drake.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
